 Craig's open seat, with markets agreeing at roughly 9-in-10 odds. Little's fundraising edge and a September staffer scandal for Pratt reinforce the tilt, though the absence of credible general-election polling keeps uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "A late shift in the national environment or a fresh Little gaffe could pull this toward a genuine tossup, since no reliable district polling exists.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Angie Craig (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's 2nd District is an open seat after Democrat Angie Craig left to run for Senate. The district leans Democratic by about three points, and the 2026 environment adds to that tilt. Matt Little won a contested DFL primary and has raised more than $900,000, while Republican Eric Pratt, a state senator, has raised about $510,000 and holds a cash-on-hand edge.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly call the race Likely or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets put Democratic win odds near 91 to 93 percent. A September resignation from Pratt's campaign over a staffer's white nationalist posts added friction on the Republican side. The race still carries real uncertainty because no high-quality general-election polling is available, but the weight of evidence favors Little.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9565, "margin": 11.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9477, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9516, "margin": 11.5}]}